Q: David, we detected that Windows Defender was disabled on your
   workstation ({{ prefix }}-DEV-Laptop-04) yesterday at 9:14 AM.
   Can you explain what happened?

A: Yeah, I disabled it. I've been working on a large C++ project and
   Defender was causing major issues with my builds. Every time I
   compile, it scans all the output files and my build times went
   from 3 minutes to over 20 minutes. It was making it impossible
   to meet my sprint deadlines.

Q: Did you submit an exclusion request through IT before making
   this change?

A: No, I didn't. I know there's a process but it takes like two
   weeks to get approved and I needed to ship a feature by Friday.
   I figured I'd just turn it off temporarily and turn it back on
   when I was done. I've done this before at my last company and
   it was never an issue.

Q: Were you aware that completely disabling Defender violates our
   security policy?

A: I mean, I knew it probably wasn't ideal, but I thought as a
   developer with local admin rights I had the authority to manage
   my own workstation. The build tools need to work. I also added
   an exclusion for my C:\DevBuilds folder thinking that would be
   a compromise.

Q: The logs show protection was off for approximately 23 hours.
   During that time, did you download any files, open emails, or
   browse the internet?

A: I was coding all day, so yeah I had browsers open for Stack
   Overflow and documentation. I probably downloaded a few NuGet
   packages and some GitHub repos. Nothing suspicious though.

Q: Going forward, what would be the correct process?

A: I guess I should have submitted the exclusion request form and
   waited for IT to add the specific build folder as an approved
   exception rather than disabling everything.

====================================================================
SECURITY TEAM NOTES:
- User admits to intentionally disabling protection
- Motivation: Build performance (compiler interference)
- User was unaware of proper exclusion request process
- Unprotected period: ~23 hours with internet access
- Correct remediation:
  1. Restore full Defender protection (done via GPO refresh)
  2. Submit proper exclusion request for C:\DevBuilds ONLY
  3. If approved, add FOLDER exclusion (not disable all protection)
  4. Do NOT allow blanket AV disablement for convenience
  5. User education on security policy compliance

POLICY REFERENCE: ACS-POL-SI-003 (Malware Protection)
  - Section 4.2: Users shall not disable endpoint protection
  - Section 5.1: Exclusion requests require IT Security approval
